 "api_error_status": null,
 "result": "Tessel's names differ from Flask's. Call order for an exception raised in a view:\n\n1. `Tessel.wsgi_entry` (`src/tessel/app.py:1566`) calls `run_full_request`.\n2. `Tessel.run_full_request` (`src/tessel/app.py:992`) calls `run_view` (`src/tessel/app.py:966`) inside a `try`. On `except Exception` it calls `route_user_fault` (line 1018).\n3. `Tessel.route_user_fault` (`src/tessel/app.py:865`):\n   - An `HTTPException` that is not trapped by `trap_http_exception` (`src/tessel/sansio/app.py:894`) goes to `route_http_fault` (`src/tessel/app.py:830`).\n   - Anything else goes to `_pick_fault_handler`. If that finds no handler, the exception is re-raised.\n4. `Tessel._pick_fault_handler` (`src/tessel/sansio/app.py:869`) picks the handler. It calls `_get_exc_class_and_code` (`src/tessel/sansio/scaffold.py:665`) to get the exception class and HTTP code. It then searches `error_handler_spec[name][code_or_None]`, walking the exception's MRO, in this order:\n   1. bundle handler for the specific code\n   2. app handler for the specific code\n   3. bundle handler for the exception class\n   4. app handler for the exception class\n5. If a handler is re-raised or none matches, the exception reaches `wsgi_entry`'s `except` and goes to `route_unhandled_fault` (`src/tessel/app.py:897`). That method logs, sends `got_request_exception`, builds an `InternalServerError`, and calls `_pick_fault_handler` once more for a 500 handler.\n\nHandlers are registered through `register_error_handler` (`src/tessel/sansio/scaffold.py:650`), which writes into `error_handler_spec`.",
